Fix two SIGSEGVs hit by short-lived CLI runs (e.g. `openrgb -c 000000 -m direct`),
most often when several instances run at once. Not upstream yet; both bugs are
still present on master 6a10f77c (2026-10-15).

1. ASUS Aura USB (mainboard, addressable, Strix LC, Ryuo): GetConfigTable() ran
   `delete this` from inside the constructor when the config-table reply did
   not match. The derived constructor and the detector then used the freed,
   half-built object -> jump to address 0 on the detection thread
   (kernel: "segfault at 0 ip 0000000000000000 ... error 14").
   The mismatch itself comes from hidraw delivering every input report to every
   open handle: a second openrgb instance's replies land in our queue. Now:
   read until the matching reply ID (bounded, 1 s timeout per report), throw on
   failure, and every detector constructing an AuraUSBController subclass
   catches it and closes the handle.

2. Logitech HID++ 2.0 receiver watcher: the watchers were stopped only by a
   static destructor defined BEFORE hidpp20_build_targets/hidpp20_building in
   LogitechControllerDetect.cpp. Statics die in reverse order, so at exit a
   worker still in HIDPP20BuildForWatcher() touched destroyed sets
   (SIGSEGV in ~HIDPP20BuildClaim / std::set::erase while main() was in
   exit() -> ~HIDPP20WatcherShutdown -> thread::join). Now the watchers are
   stopped from a new DetectionManager cleanup hook at the start of
   Cleanup() (before the controller list is cleared, so a late-built controller
   is deleted with the rest), and the static fallback object moved to the end
   of the file. Cleanup hooks are a file-scope list in DetectionManager.cpp, so
   the DetectionManager class layout seen by plugins is unchanged.

Rebased 2026-11-12: upstream moved AsusAuraUSBControllerDetect.cpp one level down,
split the ROG Strix LC and Ryuo AIO detectors into their own files and added
DetectAsusAuraUSBLiquidCoolers (AuraMainboardController), which gets the same
hid_close() on a failed constructor.

